/T (Branching and forking provide two ways of diverging from the main code line. Fork is another way of saying clone or copy. Fork is a clone on the GitLab \(or GitHub\) side \(it clones everything\). If you fork a repository, you get that repository and all of its branches. A fork is independent from the original repository If the original repository is deleted, the fork remains. )
/E (Branching and forking provide two ways of diverging from the main code line. Fork is another way of saying clone or copy. Fork is a clone on the GitLab \(or GitHub\) side \(it clones everything\). If you fork a repository, you get that repository and all of its branches. A fork is independent from the original repository If the original repository is deleted, the fork remains. )
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
